C COLLECTIONS LIBRARY - MAN PAGE TREE
======================================

This directory has the real troff/groff manual pages of the library. Each
documented symbol has its own file, except for a family of related
constants, which shares one page named after the family.


What gets a page
----------------

A symbol only gets a page if somebody needs it to USE the library, and
that's the whole test. A declaration in an installed header doesn't make a
symbol qualify, and neither does a ccol_ prefix, an export from the shared
object, or being a static inline function that a consumer could technically
call.

Before you add a page, name the user-facing task that can't be done without
that symbol. If there is no such task the symbol is internal, so it gets no
page, no section in README.md or in a guide under doc/, and no SEE ALSO
entry. You can still mention it in the prose of another page to explain what
that page's symbol does.

A name that starts with an underscore is internal, and the underscore is
there exactly to say so at every call site, so such a name never gets a
page. An internal symbol may still sit in an installed header and still be
exported, because a header can't hide what the library itself needs across
translation units. The boundary is marked by the underscore, not by the
header or the symbol table.

By that test these are internal, and none of them has a page:

    _csort_default_<type>_comparison_proc
        Every caller reaches these through csort_get_default_comparison_proc,
        which picks one from the type of its argument.

    _CCOL_CALCULATE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER_SIZE
        The size arithmetic that CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER
        performs on the caller's behalf.

    _ccol_find_nearest_gte_power_of_two, ccol_strdup, ccol_growbuf_*
        Helpers that the containers use for their own growth and storage.

A literal constant that only matters as an argument, a buffer size or a
field value of some function never gets a page of its own, even when callers
type its name. Document it, with the value it expands to, on the page of
each function or macro that takes it. A buffer length like
CHTTPSVR_PEER_CERT_SHA256_LEN goes on the page of the function that fills
the buffer, and a sentinel like CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE goes wherever the field
that accepts it is described (chttpsvr_start(3)). A set of constants that
many unrelated functions share, such as the HTTP status codes of
CHTTP_STATUS(3), keeps one page of its own so the table is only written
once.

Layout
------

    man/
      <module>/<symbol>.3   one page per raw C function or type-inferred macro
      <module>/<module>.7   the module overview page, where there is one. It
                            holds the module-wide rules that do not fit on
                            any single page
      common/ccollections.7 the overview of the whole library, with the
                            conventions that all modules share. It is named
                            after the library rather than after common.h, so
                            it installs under a name of its own

<module> is the name of the header without its .h suffix, for example
cvector, chashmap or cjson. The raw functions and their companion
type-inferred macros sit together in the same module directory. Their names
never collide, so the file system needs no rule to keep them apart, but the
".TH" line of each page records whether it documents a function or a macro:
its fourth field is either "C Collections Library - Functions" or "C
Collections Library - Macros". In both cases the SYNOPSIS shows the real
declaration, and the SYNOPSIS and EXAMPLES name each header the way an
application includes it from an install, as <ccollections/NAME.h>.

Some symbols have more than one name, like cjson_parse_mp and its
cjson_parse wrapper. Such a symbol gets one page with the content plus one
or more small ".so" redirect pages, which is how the glibc and BSD man pages
handle aliases.

A redirect page has exactly one line, ".so <module>/<symbol>.3", and nothing
else, so no ".TH" line and no comment. man renders the target page with that
page's header and footer, and mandb records the alias as a link to the
target. The NAME section of the target lists every alias so that whatis and
apropos can find each one.

A family of related constants works differently. The CHTTP_STATUS_*
constants of chttp.h are such a family: they have no behavior of their own
and a reader wants to see the whole table at once. They share one page,
man/chttp/CHTTP_STATUS.3, whose NAME section lists every constant so that
apropos and whatis can find each one. The individual names have no redirect
pages, so "man CHTTP_STATUS" opens the table but "man CHTTP_STATUS_OK"
doesn't resolve.

Viewing a page
--------------

`make install` copies every page from here into a real man3 and man7 tree
(see the root Makefile), and after that a plain `man cvector_create` works
from any directory, and so does every alias. The only exception is a
constant that shares a family page, which you reach through the name of that
page.

You can read a page without installing anything as well. Run man from
inside this directory, so that the ".so <module>/<symbol>.3" redirect of an
alias page resolves correctly, because "man -l" resolves a ".so" target
relative to the current working directory and not to where the page is.

    cd man && man -l cvector/cvector_create.3
    cd man && man -l cjson/cjson_parse.3   # an alias page, .so and all

The same pages, rendered to HTML, are on the project's GitHub Pages site at
https://abunchofnerds-25.github.io/ccollections/man/, and every link from
README.md and the guides under doc/ goes there, because GitHub shows these
files as raw troff. `make man_html` renders them into man_html/ with mandoc;
references to our own pages become links, and an alias page becomes a
redirect to the page it names. The HTML is generated and never committed:
these files stay the source.

Maintenance
-----------

When the documented behavior of a function or macro changes, edit the .3
file of that symbol directly and keep the section order of the pages around
it: NAME, LIBRARY, SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, PARAMETERS, RETURN VALUE, NOTES,
WARNINGS, THREAD SAFETY, EXAMPLES, SEE ALSO. Write every new page in plain
ASCII.

A line of text must not start with ' or ., because troff takes such a line
as a request and `man` drops it without a word. Put \& in front of it, as
in "\&.nan", and run `make man_html`, which stops on every error that
`mandoc -T lint` reports. A guide links a page by the URL of its rendered
form, https://abunchofnerds-25.github.io/ccollections/man/<module>/<page>.<n>.html,
and `make check_doc_links` refuses a link that names no page or points at
the troff file.
